 ## Overview
-Reduced Volume Texture with Box Average is a technique for handling volumetric datasets that exceed the maximum texture dimensions supported by the GPU (e.g., 2048 voxels per axis) [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering failure and main-thread stalling by maintaining multiple representations of the data: a full-resolution grid for high-quality rendering and a coarser grid created by box averaging for preview or low-resolution display [S1, S2, unofficial]. This approach allows the viewport to select the representation that fits the device's texture limits, ensuring continuous rendering even when the full volume cannot be loaded into GPU memory [S1, unofficial].
+Reduced Volume Texture with Box Average is a technique for handling volumetric datasets that exceed the maximum texture dimensions supported by the GPU (e.g., 2048 voxels per axis) [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering failure and main-thread stalling by maintaining multiple representations of the data: a full-resolution grid for high-quality rendering and a coarser grid created by box averaging for preview or low-resolution display [S1, S2, unofficial]. This approach allows the viewport to select the representation that fits the device's texture limits, ensuring continuous rendering even when the full volume cannot be loaded into GPU memory [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Multiresolution Rendering with Data Reduction](11186-multiresolution-rendering-with-data-reduction.md) and complements [ROI-based High-Resolution Rendering](13686-roi-based-high-resolution-rendering.md).
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A sequence of source frames (slices) delivered by a loader, potentially arriving asynchronously.
-*   **Output:** A reduced volume texture (coarse grid) stored in CPU memory, which is uploaded to the GPU as needed. The size of this reduced copy is bounded by the maximum texture size supported by the hardware (e.g., $256 \times 256 \times 256$ int16, which is 32 MB) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Input:** A sequence of source frames (slices) delivered by a loader, potentially arriving asynchronously [S1, S2, unofficial].
